# reverse.list — vsock_test IDs to run in the reverse direction
# (Windows = vsock acceptor / --mode=server, Linux = vsock connector / --mode=client).
#
# Row format: see forward.list.
# Contract: every enabled (id, variant) row MUST pass — failure is a regression.
# Rows are kept in numeric-ID order.
#
# --- Reverse-direction inclusion policy ---
#
# In reverse the Windows guest is the acceptor, so what a row exercises in
# our driver is whatever `.run_server` in test_cases[] actually touches.  A
# reading of the callbacks shows that surface is thin: most rows do
# `accept + one send_byte / one recv_byte + close`, and the interesting
# per-test variation lives in `.run_client` (non-blocking connect, MSG_PEEK,
# MSG_DONTWAIT, SO_LINGER, invalid buffer, connect retry) — which in reverse
# runs on the Linux side and drives our driver identically across those rows.
#
# So reverse enables ONLY rows whose `.run_server` reaches a distinct driver
# code path.  A row that is server-side just accept+trivial+close is disabled
# with the note "server-side surface duplicative", even if the client-side
# logic looks unique.  Coverage of those client-side variations belongs in
# the forward direction (where the Linux acceptor is the boring half) or in
# loopback (where both halves are on the Windows guest).
#
# Current unique reverse posix set: 1, 4, 10, 14, 21, 22.  Re-open the
# server body of another test to argue for adding one; do not re-enable
# just because a fresh driver "would probably pass" it in reverse.
#
# --- Baseline ---
#
# Current baseline: vhi-8.1 HEAD 3ec8ad6a.  Only rows known to pass on
# that baseline (and to meet the inclusion policy above) are enabled here.

# 0  posix  SOCK_STREAM connection reset  — disabled: server-side surface duplicative (accept + trivial send/recv + close); reverse coverage kept minimal (1,4,10,14,21,22 = unique driver-side paths)
0    wsa    SOCK_STREAM connection reset
0    overlapped SOCK_STREAM connection reset
1    posix  SOCK_STREAM bind only
# 2  posix  SOCK_STREAM client close  — disabled: server-side surface duplicative (accept + trivial send/recv + close); reverse coverage kept minimal (1,4,10,14,21,22 = unique driver-side paths)
2    wsa    SOCK_STREAM client close
2    overlapped SOCK_STREAM client close
# 3  posix  SOCK_STREAM server close  — disabled: server-side surface duplicative (accept + trivial send/recv + close); reverse coverage kept minimal (1,4,10,14,21,22 = unique driver-side paths)
3    wsa    SOCK_STREAM server close
3    overlapped SOCK_STREAM server close
4    posix  SOCK_STREAM multiple connections
4    wsa    SOCK_STREAM multiple connections
# 4  overlapped SOCK_STREAM multiple connections  — disabled: same fingerprint as forward — single-byte overlapped WSARecv over 100 concurrent connections completes with bytes=0.
# 5  posix  SOCK_STREAM MSG_PEEK  — disabled: server-side surface duplicative (accept + trivial send/recv + close); reverse coverage kept minimal (1,4,10,14,21,22 = unique driver-side paths)
5    wsa    SOCK_STREAM MSG_PEEK
# 5  overlapped SOCK_STREAM MSG_PEEK  — disabled: same fingerprint as forward test 4 — overlapped WSARecv completes with bytes=0 while wsa passes ("unexpected EOF while receiving bytes / bogus recv bytes read 0 (expected 32)").  Needs a targeted repro before re-enabling.
# 6  posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET msg bounds                       — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (needs SEQPACKET + recvmsg(MSG_EOR))
# 7  posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET MSG_TRUNC flag                   — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (needs SEQPACKET + recvmsg)
# 8  posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET timeout                          — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SEQPACKET not implemented in driver)
# 9  posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET invalid receive buffer           — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SEQPACKET not implemented in driver)
10   posix  SOCK_STREAM poll() + SO_RCVLOWAT
# 10 wsa    SOCK_STREAM poll() + SO_RCVLOWAT  — disabled: the wsa variant's poll is built on WSAEventSelect (edge-triggered FD_READ), which does not honour SO_RCVLOWAT; the low-water semantics are unique to WSAPoll and stay covered by the posix row (WSAPoll is our conscious posix-side exception).
# 11 posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET big message                      — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SEQPACKET not implemented in driver)
# 12 posix  SOCK_STREAM test invalid buffer  — disabled: server-side surface duplicative (accept + trivial send/recv + close); reverse coverage kept minimal (1,4,10,14,21,22 = unique driver-side paths)
# 13 posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET test invalid buffer              — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SEQPACKET not implemented in driver)
14   posix  SOCK_STREAM virtio skb merge
14   wsa    SOCK_STREAM virtio skb merge
14   overlapped SOCK_STREAM virtio skb merge
# 15 posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET MSG_PEEK                         — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SEQPACKET not implemented in driver)
# 16 posix  SOCK_STREAM SHUT_WR  — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SIGPIPE); isolated-pick harness artifact
# 17 posix  SOCK_STREAM SHUT_RD  — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SIGPIPE); isolated-pick harness artifact
# 18 posix  SOCK_STREAM MSG_ZEROCOPY                        — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (no Winsock MSG_ERRQUEUE)
# 19 posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET MSG_ZEROCOPY                     — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (no MSG_ERRQUEUE + SEQPACKET)
# 20 posix  SOCK_STREAM MSG_ZEROCOPY empty MSG_ERRQUEUE     — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (no MSG_ERRQUEUE)
21   posix  SOCK_STREAM double bind connect
22   posix  SOCK_STREAM virtio credit update + SO_RCVLOWAT
# 23 posix  SOCK_STREAM virtio credit update + low rx_bytes  — disabled: physical body passes on new TX but harness misreads due to isolated-pick control-bleed with test 24
# 24 posix  SOCK_STREAM ioctl(SIOCOUTQ) 0 unsent bytes  — disabled: server-side surface duplicative (accept + trivial send/recv + close); reverse coverage kept minimal (1,4,10,14,21,22 = unique driver-side paths)
# 25 posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET ioctl(SIOCOUTQ) 0 unsent bytes   — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SEQPACKET not implemented in driver)
# 26 posix  SOCK_STREAM leak accept queue  — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (kmemleak); isolated-pick harness artifact
# 27 posix  SOCK_STREAM MSG_ZEROCOPY leak MSG_ERRQUEUE      — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (no MSG_ERRQUEUE + kmemleak)
# 28 posix  SOCK_STREAM MSG_ZEROCOPY leak completion skb    — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (no MSG_ERRQUEUE + kmemleak)
# 29 posix  SOCK_STREAM transport release use-after-free  — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (Linux-only)
# 30 posix  SOCK_STREAM retry failed connect()  — disabled: "connect() #2: Connection reset by peer" - acceptor sends RST on retry
# 31 posix  SOCK_STREAM SO_LINGER null-ptr-deref  — disabled: server-side surface duplicative (accept + trivial send/recv + close); reverse coverage kept minimal (1,4,10,14,21,22 = unique driver-side paths)
# 32 posix  SOCK_STREAM SO_LINGER close() on unread  — disabled: "Unexpected lingering" - acceptor does not honor SO_LINGER timeout
# 33 posix  SOCK_STREAM transport change null-ptr-deref, lockdep warn  — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (transport-change); isolated-pick harness artifact
# 34 posix  SOCK_STREAM ioctl(SIOCINQ) functionality  — disabled: "Test skipped, SIOCINQ not supported" - client-side compat.h gap
# 35 posix  SOCK_SEQPACKET ioctl(SIOCINQ) functionality     — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (SEQPACKET not implemented in driver)
# 36 posix  SOCK_STREAM accept()ed socket custom setsockopt() — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (no Winsock SO_ZEROCOPY)
# 37 posix  SOCK_STREAM virtio MSG_ZEROCOPY coalescence corruption — disabled: run_client/run_server = NULL (no MSG_ERRQUEUE)
# 38 posix  SOCK_STREAM TX credit bounds                    — disabled: environmental, not a guest bug (re-classified 2026-08-21 on the Tx-refactor branch). The test checks the SENDER-side min(own,peer) TX window cap; in reverse the sender is the HOST Linux kernel, and dev008's kernel predates the upstream cap fix, so it queues the full 2MB window the server half itself advertises ("queued 2097152, expected <= 65536"). The Windows acceptor advertises correctly (forward direction passes with the guest-side cap). Re-enable on hosts with a capped kernel.
